{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RpcLcdimp8nhe6aV9nWbcp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t#!/usr/bin/env python3\n2\t\"\"\"check-substrate-drift.py — fast-lane substrate drift detector (lane C2).\n3\t\n4\tAnswers, OFFLINE by default, the three questions whose late answers cost the\n5\tframework days of latency. Measured: Claude Fable 5.1 launched 2026-09-01; the\n6\tframework adopted it explicitly in ``ab56e76`` (2026-09-02) and shipped that\n7\tadoption with v1.4.0 on 2026-09-15. ``@openai/codex`` 0.155.1 was published to\n8\tnpm on 2026-09-18 and first noticed on 2026-09-22.\n9\t\n10\t  1. CODEX — is the INSTALLED ``@openai/codex`` the version the ADR-182\n11\t     payload manifest pins (``.claude/governance/codex-cli-pin-manifest.json``\n12\t     ``package_version``)? A different version means the pair-rail's payload\n13\t     sha256 check will not match and ``check_pair_rail.py --verify-codex-pin``\n14\t     fails CLOSED at the next L3+ call. The installed version is READ, never\n15\t     probed: the binary is not executed (ADR-182 M4 — an unverified payload is\n16\t     never exec'd, ``pair-rail-gate.sh`` Gate 3a), and the drift case is\n17\t     exactly the unverified one. The launcher found on ``$PATH`` is resolved\n18\t     the way ``check_pair_rail._resolve_codex_payload`` resolves it\n19\t     (``<pkg>/bin/<launcher>`` -> ``<pkg>``); the version comes from the\n20\t     payload package ``@openai/codex-<os>-<cpu>/package.json``\n21\t     (``<version>-<os>-<cpu>``), else from ``<pkg>/package.json``. With a\n22\t     cached ``--fetch`` result it also answers: has npm ``@openai/codex``\n23\t     ``latest`` moved PAST the pin? No cache, or a cache older than\n24\t     ``--max-cache-age-days``, is a named ``unknown`` (never a silent\n25\t     \"current\"). This check compares VERSION STRINGS only; it never hashes\n26\t     the payload. An install whose version equals the pin but whose bytes\n27\t     differ reads as no finding here, while ``check_pair_rail.py\n28\t     --verify-codex-pin`` (the payload sha256 gate) says ``mismatch``. So a\n29\t     codex result with no finding is not \"pair-rail verified\"; the report's\n30\t     codex line and component say so (``_CODEX_VERSION_ONLY``).\n31\t  2. CLAUDE CODE — does the installed ``claude --version`` differ from the\n32\t     version the substrate ledger (``.claude/scripts/substrate-watch.json``,\n33\t     component ``claude_code``) was last reconciled against?\n34\t  3. MODELS — which model ids does the INSTALLED Claude Code know that the\n35\t     ADR-149 ``AVAILABLE_MODELS_WORKING_SET`` does not cover?\n36\t\n37\tModel catalog source (declared honestly). Claude Code (measured on 2.1.280,\n38\tthe darwin-arm64 build, 2026-09-22) exposes NO documented listing surface:\n39\t``claude --help`` lists no models subcommand and the settings ``model`` key\n40\tis a free string. The installed binary does, however, embed a structured\n41\tbaked catalog (layout measured on that one build only) — records of\n42\tthe shape ``{id:\"claude-…\",family:\"…\",display_name:\"…\",…}`` followed by an\n43\t``aliases:{<name>:{default:\"claude-…\"…}}`` block and a\n44\t``latest_per_family:{<family>:\"claude-…\"}`` block. This script scrapes THAT\n45\tstructure defensively (bounded regexes over a read-only mmap of the resolved\n46\tbinary) and fails OPEN with a named ``unknown`` when no record matches — a\n47\tbinary format change degrades to \"unknown\", never to a silent \"current\".\n48\t\n49\tFindings are NAMED (component, kind, severity, claim, next commands):\n50\t  severity ``drift``   — actionable divergence (``--strict`` exits 1 on these)\n51\t  severity ``info``    — context worth seeing, never a failure\n52\t  severity ``unknown`` — an input could not be observed (infra fail-open), or one\n53\t                         component's check raised (``COMPONENT_CHECK_ERROR``): that\n54\t                         component is unknown, the others' findings are kept\n55\t\n56\tAdopter shape: an input FILE that is absent makes its comparison NOT\n57\tAPPLICABLE (``info``); a file that is present but unreadable or unparseable\n58\tstays a named ``unknown``. The codex pin manifest (``.claude/governance/``)\n59\tand ADR-149 reach no adopter (install.sh ships only ``.claude/adr/README.md``;\n60\tneither directory is an upgrade target). The substrate ledger is absent after\n61\ta fresh install (install.sh copies only the ``*.sh``/``*.py``/``*.yaml`` of\n62\t``.claude/scripts/``) but present after an upgrade (the whole directory is an\n63\tupgrade target) — and then it records the MAINTAINER's reconciliation, which\n64\tthe next upgrade overwrites again. So the ledger is compared only in the\n65\tframework checkout (the pin manifest or ADR-149 is present), or when\n66\t``--ledger`` names a ledger explicitly; elsewhere it is NOT APPLICABLE.\n67\t\n68\tNetwork: NONE unless ``--fetch`` is passed. ``--fetch`` runs\n69\t``npm view @openai/codex dist-tags --json`` ONCE and caches the validated\n70\tresult under the project runtime state dir (resolved by the single resolver\n71\t``_lib/runtime_paths.py``); every later offline run reads that cache and\n72\treports its age. Executables run: ``claude --version`` (one code-defined\n73\t``[<bin>, \"--version\"]`` probe; skipped by ``--no-probe``; from ``$PATH`` or\n74\t``--claude-bin``, never from a file) and, ONLY under ``--fetch``, that one\n75\t``npm view`` (from ``$PATH`` or ``--npm-bin``). ``codex`` is never run."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c629bb59-4a63-4b18-b933-a99f884e3507","uuid":"4884cc2f-04c1-4bae-ab3d-33f2e1280838","timestamp":"2026-11-15T11:23:49.985Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.tG8bbW/tree/.claude/scripts/check-substrate-drift.py","content":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"check-substrate-drift.py — fast-lane substrate drift detector (lane C2).\n\nAnswers, OFFLINE by default, the three questions whose late answers cost the\nframework days of latency. Measured: Claude Fable 5.1 launched 2026-09-01; the\nframework adopted it explicitly in ``ab56e76`` (2026-09-02) and shipped that\nadoption with v1.4.0 on 2026-09-15. ``@openai/codex`` 0.155.1 was published to\nnpm on 2026-09-18 and first noticed on 2026-09-22.\n\n  1. CODEX — is the INSTALLED ``@openai/codex`` the version the ADR-182\n     payload manifest pins (``.claude/governance/codex-cli-pin-manifest.json``\n     ``package_version``)? A different version means the pair-rail's payload\n     sha256 check will not match and ``check_pair_rail.py --verify-codex-pin``\n     fails CLOSED at the next L3+ call. The installed version is READ, never\n     probed: the binary is not executed (ADR-182 M4 — an unverified payload is\n     never exec'd, ``pair-rail-gate.sh`` Gate 3a), and the drift case is\n     exactly the unverified one. The launcher found on ``$PATH`` is resolved\n     the way ``check_pair_rail._resolve_codex_payload`` resolves it\n     (``<pkg>/bin/<launcher>`` -> ``<pkg>``); the version comes from the\n     payload package ``@openai/codex-<os>-<cpu>/package.json``\n     (``<version>-<os>-<cpu>``), else from ``<pkg>/package.json``. With a\n     cached ``--fetch`` result it also answers: has npm ``@openai/codex``\n     ``latest`` moved PAST the pin? No cache, or a cache older than\n     ``--max-cache-age-days``, is a named ``unknown`` (never a silent\n     \"current\"). This check compares VERSION STRINGS only; it never hashes\n     the payload. An install whose version equals the pin but whose bytes\n     differ reads as no finding here, while ``check_pair_rail.py\n     --verify-codex-pin`` (the payload sha256 gate) says ``mismatch``. So a\n     codex result with no finding is not \"pair-rail verified\"; the report's\n     codex line and component say so (``_CODEX_VERSION_ONLY``).\n  2. CLAUDE CODE — does the installed ``claude --version`` differ from the\n     version the substrate ledger (``.claude/scripts/substrate-watch.json``,\n     component ``claude_code``) was last reconciled against?\n  3. MODELS — which model ids does the INSTALLED Claude Code know that the\n     ADR-149 ``AVAILABLE_MODELS_WORKING_SET`` does not cover?\n\nModel catalog source (declared honestly). Claude Code (measured on 2.1.280,\nthe darwin-arm64 build, 2026-09-22) exposes NO documented listing surface:\n``claude --help`` lists no models subcommand and the settings ``model`` key\nis a free string. The installed binary does, however, embed a structured\nbaked catalog (layout measured on that one build only) — records of\nthe shape ``{id:\"claude-…\",family:\"…\",display_name:\"…\",…}`` followed by an\n``aliases:{<name>:{default:\"claude-…\"…}}`` block and a\n``latest_per_family:{<family>:\"claude-…\"}`` block. This script scrapes THAT\nstructure defensively (bounded regexes over a read-only mmap of the resolved\nbinary) and fails OPEN with a named ``unknown`` when no record matches — a\nbinary format change degrades to \"unknown\", never to a silent \"current\".\n\nFindings are NAMED (component, kind, severity, claim, next commands):\n  severity ``drift``   — actionable divergence (``--strict`` exits 1 on these)\n  severity ``info``    — context worth seeing, never a failure\n  severity ``unknown`` — an input could not be observed (infra fail-open), or one\n                         component's check raised (``COMPONENT_CHECK_ERROR``): that\n                         component is unknown, the others' findings are kept\n\nAdopter shape: an input FILE that is absent makes its comparison NOT\nAPPLICABLE (``info``); a file that is present but unreadable or unparseable\nstays a named ``unknown``. The codex pin manifest (``.claude/governance/``)\nand ADR-149 reach no adopter (install.sh ships only ``.claude/adr/README.md``;\nneither directory is an upgrade target). The substrate ledger is absent after\na fresh install (install.sh copies only the ``*.sh``/``*.py``/``*.yaml`` of\n``.claude/scripts/``) but present after an upgrade (the whole directory is an\nupgrade target) — and then it records the MAINTAINER's reconciliation, which\nthe next upgrade overwrites again. So the ledger is compared only in the\nframework checkout (the pin manifest or ADR-149 is present), or when\n``--ledger`` names a ledger explicitly; elsewhere it is NOT APPLICABLE.\n\nNetwork: NONE unless ``--fetch`` is passed. ``--fetch`` runs\n``npm view @openai/codex dist-tags --json`` ONCE and caches the validated\nresult under the project runtime state dir (resolved by the single resolver\n``_lib/runtime_paths.py``); every later offline run reads that cache and\nreports its age. Executables run: ``claude --version`` (one code-defined\n``[<bin>, \"--version\"]`` probe; skipped by ``--no-probe``; from ``$PATH`` or\n``--claude-bin``, never from a file) and, ONLY under ``--fetch``, that one\n``npm view`` (from ``$PATH`` or ``--npm-bin``). ``codex`` is never run.","numLines":75,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1285}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RpcLcdimp8nhe6aV9nWbcp","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
